{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB08799 - MONDO_6004",
  "verbalization": "Antazoline, a histamine H1 receptor antagonist, is linked to Vasomotor Rhinitis through a series of indications involving Urticaria. The evidence suggests that Antazoline may exert a therapeutic effect on Vasomotor Rhinitis via a mechanism that involves the suppression of histamine-mediated allergic responses, which are a key component of Urticaria. This pathway is supported by the presence of multiple indirect links, all of which point to Urticaria as a common intermediate in the relationship between Antazoline and Vasomotor Rhinitis. The shared indication of Urticaria across these paths implies a potential mechanistic overlap, though the exact nature of this overlap remains unclear. The evidence is consistent with a hypothesis that Antazoline may modulate histamine signaling, potentially reducing inflammation and nasal hyperreactivity associated with Vasomotor Rhinitis. The integration of these paths supports a plausible, though not fully elucidated, mechanism by which Antazoline could be repurposed for the treatment of Vasomotor Rhinitis.",
